{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealGradingPrune","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealGradingPrune","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Archimedean","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.CommRing","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Rat"],"declarations":11,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.grade_separation_needs_nonstandard","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.grade_separation_needs_nonstandard","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L193-L206","doc":"**Exact domain of the squeeze.** Grade separation between positive\nelements is available precisely when the comparison leaves the standard\narchimedean class.\n\nThis is why the q-NS / q-Collatz / P-vs-NP bounds are legitimate uses\n(`Ω_q ≍ ε⁻¹` and `Δ_q ≍ ε³` sit at grades `+1` and `-3`, genuinely different\nclasses) and why the volume target is outside the domain (all of\n`Vol(4₁)`, `Vol(5₂)`, `Vol(𝓑₃)` and every `P(V)` sit at grade `0`).\n\nA route that manufactures a genuinely non-standard object whose standard part\nis the relation is *not* pruned by this file. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.grade_aeval_const","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.grade_aeval_const","kind":"theorem","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L178-L189","doc":"Every non-vanishing polynomial value at real arguments carries the **same**\ngrade. So the grading assigns one value to every non-relation and one to every\nrelation: it is exactly the characteristic function of the conjecture, and\nsupplies no independent information about which polynomials vanish.\n\nThis is `no_separation_of_standard` in the shape of the actual target: `P`\nranges over `ℚ[x,y,z]` and `v = ![Vol(4₁), Vol(5₂), Vol(𝓑₃)]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.grade_blind_to_rationality","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.grade_blind_to_rationality","kind":"theorem","line":166,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L166-L174","doc":"**The grading cannot separate a rational from an irrational.**\n\nA falsification test for the whole transfer: the corpus's own ladder starts at\n`SingleVolumeIrrational` (`Vol(4₁) ∉ ℚ`), which is *weaker* than algebraic\nindependence. The mechanism cannot reach that rung either — rationals and\nirrationals share the single archimedean class of `ℝ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.nonvanishing_premise_circular","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.nonvanishing_premise_circular","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L149-L164","doc":"**The non-vanishing premise is circular.**\n\nAdopt the usual bottom convention `ordω(0) = -∞` (`hbot`). Then the grade\npremise that would certify `x ≠ 0` by dominance is *logically equivalent* to\n`x ≠ 0`. Any grade-dominance proof of non-vanishing therefore consumes its own\nconclusion as its input — the `ω`-adic notation restates the question.\n\nApplied to `x = P(Vol(4₁), Vol(5₂), Vol(𝓑₃))`: writing the conjecture as a\ngrade statement is a change of notation, not a step of the proof. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.no_separation_of_standard","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.no_separation_of_standard","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L138-L147","doc":"**The squeeze proves no inequality between standard reals.**\n\nThe mechanism concludes `b < a` from the premise `g b < g a`. For positive\nstandard `a`, `b` that premise is *false* — so the mechanism yields no strict\ninequality between standard reals at all. This is the direct obstruction: the\nindependence target `|P(V)| > 0` is a separation among standard reals. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.grade_eq_of_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.grade_eq_of_pos","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L124-L134","doc":"**The grading is constant on the positive reals.**\n\nDerived, not assumed: if two positive reals had different grades, dominance\nwould make one infinitely smaller than the other, contradicting\n`not_infinitelySmaller_of_pos`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.not_infinitelySmaller_of_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.not_infinitelySmaller_of_pos","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L109-L122","doc":"**No positive real is infinitely smaller than any real** — the archimedean\nproperty of `ℝ`, in the form the prune needs. This is the invariant that makes\nthe exclusion below scope-(b) rather than a failed search.\n\nPositivity of `b` is not required: for `b ≤ 0` the `n = 0` instance of\n`InfinitelySmaller` already fails. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.SqueezeGrading.dominance","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.SqueezeGrading.dominance","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L105-L105","doc":"Grade dominance: a strict grade drop is an archimedean-class drop. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.SqueezeGrading.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.SqueezeGrading.mk","kind":"ctor","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L103-L103","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.SqueezeGrading","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.SqueezeGrading","kind":"structure","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L95-L105","doc":"**A grading in the shape the surreal-squeeze arguments consume.**\n\nThe single field is the soundness property those arguments actually use: a\nstrictly smaller grade means infinitely smaller, which is what licenses\nreading `ordω(cost) > ordω(budget)` off as `cost > budget`.\n\nNothing here assumes the grading is trivial on `ℝ` — that is *derived*\n(`grade_eq_of_pos`) from this field plus the archimedean property. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.InfinitelySmaller","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SurrealPrune.InfinitelySmaller","kind":"def","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/SurrealGradingPrune.lean#L87-L93","doc":"`InfinitelySmaller a b` — `a` is *infinitely smaller* than `b`: every\nstandard multiple of `a` stays strictly below `b`.\n\nThis is the Conway–Gonshor archimedean-class comparison. A strict `ordω` drop\nmeans exactly this, which is what makes the squeeze's grade-dominance step\nsound. ","signatureChunk":0}]}